# .gitattributes — line-ending policy for OpenEar
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (do not delete):
# OpenEar was built Windows-only and its history was committed from a Windows box
# with core.autocrlf=true, so every blob in the repo is stored LF. When the project
# was opened on macOS for the first time (2026-07-29) the working tree that Dropbox
# had synced from Windows was CRLF on disk, and git reported 2,902 changed lines
# across 12 files that contained zero real content changes.
#
# Pinning the policy here makes it structural instead of dependent on how each
# machine's git happens to be configured.

# Default: git normalizes text to LF in the repository, and checks it out LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# CRITICAL — Windows shell scripts MUST be checked out CRLF on EVERY platform.
# cmd.exe cannot parse an LF .bat file: labels and goto break, execution falls
# through into whatever branch follows, and variables come back empty. A blank
# port variable once turned a targeted taskkill into a machine-wide one and
# crashed Jonathan's PC (see rules.md #12). Windows PowerShell 5.1 has the same
# class of problem reading BOM-less script bytes. These two lines are the wall
# that keeps an LF .bat from ever reaching Windows again.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets — never convert, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.bin binary
*.pt binary
*.onnx binary
